+++
title = "User Journey"
description = "A guided tour of a Revela project, from an empty folder to a published site and beyond."
template = "docs"
+++

This tour follows one project from an empty folder to a published website, and then through the changes you'll make over the following months.

> **Setup:** The examples use **Windows** and the **Standalone** edition (see [Installation](../installation/)). Every step works the same on macOS and Linux; only the paths look different.
>
> You can do everything from the **interactive menu** (run `revela` without arguments) or with **commands**. Both are shown. The menu groups commands under **Build**, **Content**, **Setup**, **Addons** and **Info**. The [CLI Reference](../../reference/cli-reference/) lists every command.

## 1. Create Your Project

Revela treats the folder you start it in as your project. Create an empty folder and start Revela there:

```powershell
mkdir D:\Photos\MyPortfolio
cd D:\Photos\MyPortfolio
revela
```

Revela finds no `project.json` and offers to create a project:

```
╭─Create Project───────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ No Project Found                                             │
│                                                              │
│ This directory (MyPortfolio) doesn't contain a Revela        │
│ project. Would you like to create one?                       │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

What would you like to do?
> Create New Project
  Skip (use menu instead)
  Exit
```

Choose **Create New Project**. The project wizard asks five things:

1. **Project settings**: a display name (spaces allowed) and, optionally, the URL your site will live at
2. **Directory paths**: where your photos are (`source`) and where the site goes (`output`)
3. **Theme**: Lumina
4. **Image settings**: output formats and quality. The defaults are AVIF 75, WebP 85 and JPG 90.
5. **Site metadata**: title, author, description, copyright

After that it offers optional steps, for example connecting a [OneDrive folder](../../plugins/source-onedrive/). Skip them for now.

> **Tip:** `source` can point anywhere, for example `D:\OneDrive\Photos\Portfolio`. Revela reads your photos there without copying them. You can change the paths later with `revela config paths`.

Your folder now contains:

```
D:\Photos\MyPortfolio\
├── project.json    # Project settings (paths, theme, image formats)
└── site.json       # Site metadata (title, author, ...)
```

## 2. Add Your Photos

Create a `source\` folder and copy your photos into it. **Folders become galleries**, and the number prefix sets the order:

```
D:\Photos\MyPortfolio\source\
└── 01 Galleries\
    ├── 2024 Vacation\
    │   ├── beach.jpg
    │   └── sunset.jpg
    └── 2025 Wedding\
        ├── cake.jpg
        ├── church.jpg
        └── party.jpg
```

Top-level folders such as `01 Galleries` become navigation sections, and their subfolders become galleries. Numbers are stripped from titles and URLs, so `2024 Vacation` ends up at `/galleries/2024-vacation/`. See [Source Structure](../../guide/source-structure/) for filter galleries, text pages and more.

## 3. Check and Build

Before your first build, let Revela check the project:

```bash
revela check
```

It reports configuration, structure, theme and content problems together with a suggested fix. Errors make `revela check` exit with code `2`; hints (such as "no `baseUrl` configured") and warnings are just information.

Now build the site: **Build → generate → all** in the menu, or:

```bash
revela generate all
```

The pipeline runs five steps:

```
Pipeline: scan → calendar → statistics → pages → images

━━━ Step 1/5: scan ━━━
Scan content and update manifest

╭─Success──────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Content scan complete!                           │
│                                                  │
│ Project:    My Photography Portfolio             │
│                                                  │
│ Statistics:                                      │
│   Galleries:  2                                  │
│   Images:     5                                  │
│   Navigation: 4                                  │
│   Duration:   0.50s                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

━━━ Step 2/5: calendar ━━━
Generate availability calendar from iCal data
(no calendar pages yet: skipped)

━━━ Step 3/5: statistics ━━━
Generate statistics JSON from EXIF data
(no statistics pages yet: skipped)

━━━ Step 4/5: pages ━━━
Generate HTML pages from manifest

╭─Success─────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Page rendering complete!                    │
│                                             │
│ Statistics:                                 │
│   Pages:    8                               │
│   Duration: 0.20s                           │
╰─────────────────────────────────────────────╯

━━━ Step 5/5: images ━━━
Process images from manifest

Encoding photos  ████████████████████  5/5 (100%)  ·  2s elapsed
⠋ 8 workers busy · avif 5 · webp 5 · jpg 5

╭─Success──────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Image processing complete!                   │
│                                              │
│ Statistics:                                  │
│   Processed: 5 images                        │
│   Files:     123 created                     │
│   Size:      2.1 MB (generated)              │
│   Duration:  2.57s                           │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────╯

✓ Pipeline completed in 3.36s
```

The output is a complete static website:

```
D:\Photos\MyPortfolio\
├── .cache\              # Manifest: what was scanned and processed
├── output\
│   ├── index.html
│   ├── galleries\
│   │   ├── 2024-vacation\index.html
│   │   └── 2025-wedding\index.html
│   ├── photo\           # One page per photo
│   ├── images\
│   │   └── galleries\2024-vacation\beach\
│   │       ├── 160.avif  160.webp  160.jpg
│   │       ├── 320.avif  320.webp  320.jpg
│   │       └── ...       # every theme size, plus the original width
│   └── _assets\         # Theme CSS, JavaScript and fonts
└── source\
```

Each photo is saved in every size the theme asks for that is smaller than the photo, plus its original width, in each enabled format. See [Image Processing](../../guide/images/).

## 4. Preview

Choose **Build → serve**, or run:

```bash
revela serve
```

```
🌐 Serving D:\Photos\MyPortfolio\output at http://localhost:8080
   Press Ctrl+C to stop
```

Open `http://localhost:8080` in your browser. The server does not watch files: after a change, run `revela generate all` again and refresh the page. See [Dev Server](../../plugins/serve/).

## 5. Everyday Changes

Just run `revela generate all` again after any change. Revela keeps a manifest in `.cache\` and only redoes what's needed.

### Edit, add or remove photos

- **Edited a photo** (re-exported from Lightroom, for example): only that photo is encoded again.
- **Added a gallery**: only the new photos are encoded; the others are reported as cached.
- **Deleted a photo**: it disappears from the pages, but its generated images stay in `output\images\`. Remove them with:

  ```bash
  revela clean images --dry-run   # preview
  revela clean images             # delete
  ```

### Change image quality or formats

Run `revela config image` (menu: **Setup → config → image**) and set, for example, AVIF to 70. On the next build, Revela notices the change and re-encodes **only the AVIF files**. JPG and WebP stay as they are, and you don't need `--force`.

Enabling a new format creates just that format. Disabling a format stops creating it; `revela clean images` removes the old files.

### Change image sizes

Sizes belong to the theme, in its `Configuration/images.json`. The Lumina defaults are:

```json
{
  "sizes": [160, 320, 480, 640, 720, 960, 1280, 1440, 1920, 2560],
  "resizeMode": "longest"
}
```

To use your own sizes, extract just the size file into your project and edit `themes\Lumina\Configuration\images.json`:

```bash
revela theme extract Lumina --file Configuration/images.json
```

If you add `3840`, the next build creates a 3840 variant for every photo that is wider than 3840 pixels. Nothing else is encoded again. If you remove a size, `revela clean images` deletes its files. See [Themes](../../guide/themes/).

### Output folder deleted

If `output\` (or part of it) disappears, the next `revela generate all` recreates whatever is missing.

## 6. Add an About Page

Create a text page (menu: **Content → create → page → text**):

```bash
revela create page text "02 About" --title "About Me"
```

This creates `source\02 About\_index.revela`. Open it in a text editor and write your content in Markdown below the front matter:

```toml
+++
title = "About Me"
template = "page"
+++
## Hello!

I'm a landscape and travel photographer based in Berlin.
```

Rebuild, and the page appears in the navigation. Add `hidden = true` to keep a page (such as an imprint) out of the navigation. See [Creating Pages](../../guide/pages/).

## 7. Add a Statistics Page

Revela can show which cameras, lenses and settings you use most:

```bash
revela create page statistics "03 Statistics" --title "Photo Statistics"
```

The page uses the `statistics/overview` template. On the next `generate all`, the **statistics** step reads the EXIF data of your photos, and the page shows it as bar charts. See [Statistics](../../plugins/statistics/).

## 8. Add a Favicon

Put your favicon files in `source\_static\favicon\` (everything in `_static\` is copied to the output root) and override the theme's `Favicon.revela` partial. [Static Files](../../guide/source-structure/static-files/) explains the steps.

## 9. Publish

Your `output\` folder is the finished website. Upload it to any static host: GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages or your own web space. Set the site's public address first so Revela can generate `sitemap.xml`:

```bash
revela config project --url https://example.com
```

See [Deployment](../../guide/deployment/) for step-by-step instructions and a ready-made GitHub Actions workflow.

## 10. Months Later

Come back, drop new photos into `source\` and run `revela generate all`. Revela remembers what it already processed, so only the new photos are encoded.

To start completely fresh, delete everything Revela generated and rebuild:

```bash
revela clean all
revela generate all
```

## When Something Goes Wrong

Start with:

```bash
revela check
```

It lists every problem it finds, with the file and a suggested fix, and exits with code `2` if it finds an error. To run a single area, use `revela check config`, `structure`, `theme`, `content`, `slugs` or `calendar`.

## Summary: What Triggers Work

| You change… | Revela does… |
|-------------|--------------|
| Add a photo | Encodes all variants of the new photo |
| Edit a photo (file size or date changes) | Re-encodes that photo |
| Delete a photo | Removes it from pages; `clean images` removes its files |
| Add a size to `images.json` | Creates only the new size |
| Enable a format | Creates only the new format |
| Change a format's quality | Re-encodes only that format |
| Change the theme's `resizeMode` | Re-encodes all photos |
| Update Revela with a new image pipeline | Re-encodes all photos |
| Delete generated files | Recreates the missing files |
| `revela generate images --force` | Re-encodes everything |
